{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"WHO matters?","sub":"Session 1 live class · From order taker to business partner","note":"Open the call five minutes early and greet people by name as they join. Once the sponsor has joined, hand over for their ten-minute message: what they need from Procurement. Ask them to be concrete and to stay for one question from the room. Then thank them and move to the agenda."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How we work"],["09:00","Welcome, sponsor, check-in","Plenary"],["09:20","1.1 Reality check","Poll and debate"],["09:55","1.2 What strategic Procurement means","Debate"],["10:25","Break",""],["10:35","1.3 The internal client ecosystem","Debate and pairs"],["11:10","1.4 Prioritise and segment","Breakout scoring"],["11:55","Break",""],["12:05","1.5 The person behind the stakeholder","Pairs role-play"],["12:35","Case 1, fieldwork and close","Trios, then plenary"]],"note":"Clock times assume a 09:00 start; shift them if your class starts later. Say that the videos covered the theory, so today is about their own cases, disagreement and decisions. Agree the ground rules in one sentence: cameras on, chat welcome at any time, breakout trios stay the same for the whole programme. Keep this slide under two minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Energiser: finish the sentence","q":"\"Procurement is the department that...\" Finish it the way your toughest internal client would.","b":["Type it, but do not press Enter yet","Everyone sends on my count of three","Be honest: nobody here is judged"],"min":6,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Count down and ask everyone to send at the same moment, so nobody copies. Read five or six answers aloud, including the harsh ones, and laugh with the room rather than defending Procurement. Ask one person whose answer stood out: what happened to make your client say that? Close with: today we decide whose sentence we want to change first."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l1","note":"Launch the poll and give 30 seconds. Expect most votes on the first two options; if anyone picks 'a partner we call early', ask them what they did to get there, because that is the target for everyone else. Keep the result visible and say we will come back to it at the end of the programme."},{"k":"section","n":"1.1","h":"Reality check","sub":"How do our requests really start?","note":"Remind them the video lesson introduced the influence curve; we now test it against their own pre-work list of ten requests. Ask them to open that list now. Allow 35 minutes for the whole module."},{"k":"flow","h":"Value is set early; we usually arrive late","s":["Idea","Budget","Requirement","Supplier shortlist","Negotiation","Contract","Purchase order"],"p":"Specification and supplier choice set the price. Negotiation only moves it.","note":"Walk the stages left to right and ask: what can we still change at each one? By negotiation it is only price and terms; at purchase order it is almost nothing. Ask for one example from the room where they changed a specification rather than a price. Two minutes, then go to the poll."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l2","note":"Ask them to answer from their actual list, not their impression. Read out the distribution and write down the share that chose 'idea or budget' or 'requirement': that is the room's early involvement rate and it is measured again at the capstone. Also check the workbook submissions for Practice 1.1 if people entered their requests before class."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Whose fault is it?","q":"\"We are called late because the business does not respect Procurement.\"","b":["Their reasons: their budget, we seem slow, urgency","Our reasons: we wait, we bring process, not insight","Which list is longer in your own area?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Ask people to show agree or disagree with a reaction or raised hand, then call on one from each side. Most rooms start by blaming the business; push gently with: how many of your last ten could you have seen coming? Steer the conclusion towards what we control: showing up first, with data. Do not let it become a complaint session; cut at ten minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Which request did you see coming?","q":"Pick one of your last ten requests that a budget, a contract expiry or a known project could have predicted.","b":["What signal was there, and when?","Who knew before you did?","What would you have done with six more months?"],"min":12,"format":"Pairs in breakout rooms","note":"Send pairs to breakout rooms for seven minutes; each person tells one story. Back in plenary, take two stories and write the signals in the chat: budget rounds, expiry dates, project portfolios. Remind them that in a mature function 60 to 70% of the sourcing plan comes from contracts already known to expire, and that when the Group asked teams to list next year's initiatives, most struggled to name ten. Ask: could you list your top ten for the next three months today?"},{"k":"section","n":"1.2","h":"What strategic Procurement means","sub":"From \"What do you need me to buy?\" to \"What will you need?\"","note":"This module takes 30 minutes. The point is not definitions; it is to make each person look at where their own time goes and decide one thing to change."},{"k":"table","h":"Three levels, three questions","t":[["Level","The question we ask","How the business sees us"],["Transactional","What do you need me to buy?","A mandatory step"],["Tactical","How can I source this better?","A useful service when asked"],["Strategic","What will you need, and what should we do together?","A partner from the start"]],"note":"Read only the middle column aloud, slowly, as three sentences. Ask: which of these questions did you actually ask a stakeholder last week? Note that all three levels are needed; the problem is when the first one fills the whole week."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l3","note":"Expect transactional work and firefighting to dominate. Ask someone who voted 'strategic work' what that work looked like concretely. Link to Practice 1.2 in the workbook: they split their month today and set a target for twelve months from now; ask them to do it after class if they have not yet."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is strategy a luxury?","q":"\"With our workload, strategic Procurement is a luxury we cannot afford.\"","b":["What would you stop doing to make room?","Which urgent renewals were planning failures?","Strategic work makes transactional peaks smaller: true?"],"min":10,"format":"Open floor","note":"Let the sceptics speak first; they are often right about capacity. Then ask: which of your urgencies last quarter were created by not planning? The key line to land is that strategic work is different work, not more work, because planned renewals do not become emergencies. Ask two people to name one transactional task they could automate or plan away."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 10:35. Think about who really decides in your main spend area.","note":"Put a visible countdown in the chat. Before leaving, plant the question for 1.3 so people come back thinking about their own stakeholders. Restart on time even if a few are missing."},{"k":"section","n":"1.3","h":"The internal client ecosystem","sub":"A stakeholder is rarely one person","note":"This module takes 35 minutes. The video showed the full role table for Nordvia IT; here we test it on the trickier cases and on their own areas."},{"k":"bullets","h":"Who holds each role around a spend area?","b":["Budget owner and demand owner","Specification owner and service owner","Supplier relationship owner","Decision maker","Influencers and gatekeepers"],"note":"Ask: in your main area, are budget owner and demand owner the same person? Usually not, and the person who sends the request is often neither. Use Nordvia Marketing as the contrast case: the CMO decides largely alone, while each brand manager owns 'their' agency relationship. Also ask: what role does Procurement play today in that map?"},{"k":"discuss","h":"Gatekeepers: obstacles or allies?","q":"\"Risk, Compliance and Legal are Procurement's best allies, not obstacles.\"","b":["Solvency II outsourcing and DORA raise their voice","Who in the room works closely with them already?","What could we bring them early?"],"min":8,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"In insurance, outsourcing rules under Solvency II and the DORA rules for ICT third-party providers make Risk, Compliance and Information Security key influencers. Ask who sees them as a brake and who has used them as a door-opener to the business. Land the idea that a buyer who understands these rules becomes useful to the business early."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Where are your question marks?","q":"Which role in your own area can you not name today?","b":["Name the area and the missing role","Who could tell you the answer?","Turn it into one fieldwork question"],"min":10,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask them to sketch their own map (Practice 1.3 in the workbook) for three minutes, then post their biggest question mark in the chat. Group the answers aloud: the decision maker and the influencers are usually the most common gaps. Tell them each question mark is a question for the discovery interview in their fieldwork."},{"k":"section","n":"1.4","h":"Prioritise and segment stakeholders","sub":"Where do we invest limited capacity?","note":"This is the longest module, 45 minutes, and it produces the key decision of the day: one priority stakeholder. Remind them of the scoring model from the video and keep the explanation to three minutes."},{"k":"two","h":"Two axes, six criteria, scored 1 to 5","l":{"t":"Value potential","b":["Addressable spend 30%","Value opportunity 25%","Future demand 20%","Criticality and risk 25%"]},"r":{"t":"Relationship today","b":["Access and trust 50%","Timing of involvement 50%","3 or more counts as high"]},"note":"Ask the room: which criterion is the hardest to score honestly? Usually it is relationship, because we overrate friendly contacts. Clarify that value opportunity means things like many suppliers, no frameworks, expiring contracts and prices never benchmarked."},{"k":"cards","h":"Four segments, one priority","c":[["Develop","High value, weak relationship: the priority of this programme"],["Strategic partner","High value, strong relationship: joint pipeline"],["Maintain","Lower value, strong relationship: periodic check-ins"],["Serve efficiently","Lower value, weak relationship: catalogues, self-service"]],"note":"Point out that Develop is the priority because that is where the value is and where a better relationship changes the most. Mention a lens from our own Group: IT and BPO and professional services make up about two thirds of addressable spend, and IT is the largest category in nine of ten entities. So IT and Marketing leaders are often high value and call us latest."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Score the six Nordvia clients","min":25,"where":"Breakout trios, then Module 1.4 in your workbook","b":["Use the Nordvia client data in the Case tab","Agree a value and relationship score for each","Note where your trio disagreed, and why","Then score five of your own stakeholders"],"note":"Send the fixed trios to breakout rooms: 15 minutes on Nordvia, 10 minutes on their own stakeholders. Visit each room briefly and ask what evidence they used. Most teams land IT and Marketing in Develop; Facilities and Communication cause the most disagreement, which is what we want to debrief."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s1-l4","note":"Run the poll as soon as trios return. If the votes split between IT and Marketing, ask one trio from each side to defend their choice in 30 seconds. If someone voted General Services, ask whether they chose it for value or because the relationship is easy. That sets up the next debate."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Friendly or important?","q":"\"Your priority stakeholder should be the one who calls you last.\"","b":["Is a friendly stakeholder a wasted programme?","How much risk can you take in eight weeks?","What evidence would change your choice?"],"min":12,"format":"Open floor","note":"Participants tend to choose a friendly stakeholder. Push them, kindly, towards a high-value one in the Develop segment, while accepting that a deputy of that stakeholder is a fair starting point. Close by asking everyone to write their priority stakeholder and one reason in the workbook before the break."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 12:05. Write down your priority stakeholder's name first.","note":"Check that most people have written a priority stakeholder; if several have not, give them a minute after the break. Restart on time."},{"k":"section","n":"1.5","h":"The person behind the stakeholder","sub":"Start from their goals, not ours","note":"This module takes 30 minutes. Stakeholders invite people who help them reach their own goals, so we describe the priority stakeholder in their own terms before we propose anything."},{"k":"cards","h":"The persona canvas in four questions","c":[["Their world","Accountability, KPIs, priorities and pressures"],["Their success","What a good year looks like for them"],["Their way of working","Decisions they control; data, stories or speed"],["Their view of us","Frustrations, and why they should give us an hour"]],"note":"The workbook canvas has nine boxes; this slide groups them into four so it is easy to talk to. Insist on the 'assumed' tick: anything they are guessing becomes an interview question. Ask: how many of you could fill 'their KPIs' without guessing?"},{"k":"discuss","h":"Would you give Procurement an hour?","q":"You are the stakeholder. Your buyer asks for an hour. What would make you say yes?","b":["Partner A plays the stakeholder, B asks","Swap after five minutes","Note what you had to assume"],"min":15,"format":"Pairs in breakout rooms","note":"Each pair uses one person's priority stakeholder, and the 'stakeholder' answers in character from the persona canvas. Back in plenary, ask: what reason to meet actually worked? Usually it is a concrete topic of theirs, such as a budget pressure or a contract that worries them, not a Procurement process."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Case 1: the invisible Procurement team","min":15,"where":"Breakout trios, Case tab in your workbook","b":["Nordvia wants €5M savings from €186M indirect spend","What is really wrong?","Which stakeholders come first, and why?","What would you do in the next 90 days?"],"note":"Nordvia Insurance is fictional. Trios have already scored the clients, so they can go straight to diagnosis and a 90-day plan; one person types the team answer. Warn them they will present in two minutes each, so keep it to three points."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Problem or symptom?","q":"\"Nordvia's real problem is low savings.\"","b":["Each trio: two minutes, three points","Peer challenge: one question per team","What information is missing?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"After the trio presentations, put the statement to the room. The answer we want them to reach: low savings are a symptom of late involvement and no view of demand. Ask the other trios to challenge each plan with one question, and protect the presenters from pile-ons."},{"k":"bullets","h":"Fieldwork before Session 2","b":["Confirm your priority stakeholder with your manager","Build the data pack: spend, contracts, expiries","Hold a 30-minute discovery interview","Write down one thing that surprised you"],"note":"Point them to the interview guide in the workbook and stress the rule: listen more than you talk, and present nothing yet. The data pack covers 24 months of spend by supplier and category, active contracts with values and expiry dates, and Group frameworks that could apply. Ask each person to put the interview date in their calendar before leaving the call."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"What we take away today","b":["Value is decided early; we usually arrive late","Most of our future work is predictable","Strategic work is different work, not more work","A stakeholder is a set of roles","Invest first where value is high and access weak"],"note":"Ask each person to type in the chat one word for how they feel about their fieldwork. Thank the sponsor if they are still on the call. End on time; stay five minutes for anyone who wants to check their choice of priority stakeholder."}],"polls":[{"id":"s1-l1","q":"How would your internal clients describe Procurement today?","options":["A mandatory step","A useful service when asked","A partner we call early","They rarely think of us"]},{"id":"s1-l2","q":"In your last ten requests, at which stage were you MOST OFTEN first contacted?","options":["Idea or budget","Requirement","Supplier shortlist","Negotiation","Contract or purchase order"]},{"id":"s1-l3","q":"Where does most of your working month go today?","options":["Transactional work","Tactical sourcing","Strategic work","Firefighting urgencies"]},{"id":"s1-l4","q":"After scoring: which Nordvia internal client should Procurement invest in first?","options":["IT","Marketing","Facilities","General Services","Communication"]}]}